---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Showcase from "../../../components/Showcase.astro";

const install = `pnpm add @skryensya/core @skryensya/vanilla @skryensya/react`;

const html = `<input
  class="sk-slider"
  type="range"
  min="0"
  max="100"
  value="65"
  data-sk-slider
  aria-label="Volumen"
  style="--sk-slider-fill: 0.65"
/>
<input
  class="sk-slider"
  type="range"
  min="0"
  max="100"
  value="30"
  disabled
  aria-label="Brightness (disabled)"
  style="--sk-slider-fill: 0.3"
/>`;

const react = `import { Slider } from "@skryensya/react/slider";

<Slider
  aria-label="Volumen"
  defaultValue={65}
  onValueChange={setVolume}
/>
<Slider aria-label="Brillo" defaultValue={30} disabled />;`;
---

<Base title="Slider" description="Slider: native range with skin, painted fill without JavaScript and React component.">
  <h1>Slider</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Slider is a real <code>&lt;input type="range"&gt;</code> with skin: keyboard, participation in
    Forms and accessibility tree come from the platform. The filled portion is displayed as
    the <code>--sk-slider-fill</code> property (0–1): the initial value is painted by the CSS alone, and continue
    the thumb while dragging is the only thing the binding needs.
  </p>

  <Showcase html={html} react={react} label="Slider" />
  <p>In Vanilla, <code>data-sk-slider</code> records the minimum binding that rewrites <code>--sk-slider-fill</code> in each <code>input</code>, nothing more: keyboard, focus and form remain platform-specific. In React, <code>&lt;Slider&gt;</code> keeps the property in sync in controlled and uncontrolled mode, and reports the change as a number via <code>onValueChange</code>.</p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={install} label="terminal" />

  <h2>React</h2>
  <p>The code is in the <strong>React</strong> tab of the preview.</p>
</Base>
